From <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;substack.com&#x2F;@generativehistory" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;substack.com&#x2F;@generativehistory</a>:
Mark Humphries identifies himself as &quot;Professor of History at Wilfrid Laurier University&quot;<p>From article:
&quot;The only scholarly reference I could find to his 1808 trip to Canada was in fur trader George Nelson’s journals, published by Harry Duckworth as Friends, Foes, and Furs in 2019. In his journal, Nelson mentioned hearing that a “Count de Chabot” had visited Fort William and so, as editor of the text, Duckworth provided a paragraph of context for readers. Dr. Duckworth has an encyclopedic knowledge of fur trade sources, but neither he nor any of the other experts I’ve shared this finding with have given Chabot much thought, if they’ve even heard of him.&quot;
